Q.What is FailFast and FailSafe. Difference between the both , in which scenario are they used
and what is the consequence of them.

🔷 1️⃣ What is Fail-Fast?
👉
Fail-Fast means: if a collection is modified while iterating, it immediately throws an exception.

🔹 Example

import java.util.*;

List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add(1);
list.add(2);

for (Integer i : list) {
    list.add(3); // ❌ modifying while iterating
}

❗ Output

ConcurrentModificationException

🔹 Why does this happen?

👉 Internally:

(i)Collection keeps a modCount
(ii)Iterator checks it

👉 If modified:

❌ Throw exception immediately

🔹 Where used?

1.ArrayList
2.HashMap
3.HashSet

👉 Most collections in java.util

🔷 2️⃣ What is Fail-Safe?
👉
Fail-Safe means: you can modify the collection while iterating without exception.

🔹 Example

import java.util.concurrent.*;

CopyOnWriteArrayList<Integer> list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
list.add(1);
list.add(2);

for (Integer i : list) {
    list.add(3); // ✅ allowed
}

✔ Output

No exception

🔹 Why?

👉 It works on:

1.Copy of collection
2.Not original

🔹 Where used?

1.CopyOnWriteArrayList
2.ConcurrentHashMap

👉 Package:

java.util.concurrent

🔷 3️⃣ Key Difference

Feature	                                   Fail-Fast	                         Fail-Safe

Modification during iteration	           ❌ Not allowed	                     ✅ Allowed
Exception	                               Yes	                                  No
Works on	                               Original collection	                  Copy
Performance	                               Faster	                              Slower
Thread-safe	                               ❌ No	                              ✅ Yes

🔷 4️⃣ Consequences

❌ Fail-Fast
(i)   Throws ConcurrentModificationException
(ii)  Stops execution
(iii) Helps detect bugs early

⚠️ Fail-Safe
(i)  No exception
But:
(ii)  May not reflect latest data
(iii) Works on snapshot

🔷 5️⃣ Benefits of each
✅ Fail-Fast Advantages

✔ Detects bugs immediately
✔ Better performance
✔ No extra memory

❗ Disadvantages

❌ Not thread-safe
❌ Cannot modify during iteration

✅ Fail-Safe Advantages

✔ Safe in multithreading
✔ No exception
✔ Allows modification

❗ Disadvantages

❌ Extra memory (copy)
❌ Slightly slower
❌ May work on outdated data

🔷 6️⃣ Real-life analogy

Fail-Fast:

You are reading a book
Someone changes pages → you stop immediately ❌

Fail-Safe:

You are reading a photocopy
Original changes → no effect ✅

🔷 🔥 Important Interview Point

👉
Fail-Fast is not truly guaranteed — it’s “best effort”

🔷 🔥 Final Interview Answer
👉
Fail-Fast collections throw a ConcurrentModificationException if modified during iteration,
helping detect bugs early. Fail-Safe collections work on a copy of the data and allow
modifications without exceptions, making them suitable for concurrent environments but with
additional memory overhead.

🔷 One-line memory trick
👉
Fail-Fast → fail immediately
Fail-Safe → work on copy